electric-energy-density.interpretation:
The equation $u_E = \frac{1}{2} 
e_0 E^2$ describes the electric energy density. Here, $u_E$ represents the electric energy density, measured in Joules per cubic meter ($J/m^3$). The constant $
e_0$ is the vacuum permittivity, a fundamental constant of nature with a value of approximately $8.854 	imes 10^{-12} \frac{F}{m}$ (Farads per meter), which characterizes how an electric field affects and is affected by vacuum. The term $E$ represents the magnitude of the electric field, measured in Newtons per Coulomb ($N/C$) or Volts per meter ($V/m$). The factor of $\frac{1}{2}$ arises from integrating the energy required to build up the electric field from zero to its final strength. The $E^2$ term indicates that the energy density increases quadratically with the electric field strength, meaning that doubling the electric field strength quadruples the energy density.
